WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:01.760
Hey, what's up everyone?

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:05.608
My name is Luv Aggarwal, and I’m a Data
Platform Solution engineer for IBM. 

00:00:06.113 --> 00:00:06.991
Machine Learning.

00:00:07.096 --> 00:00:11.269
There's no doubt that this is an incredibly
hot topic with significant interest from both

00:00:11.269 --> 00:00:16.219
business professionals as well as technologists.
So let's talk about what machine learning,

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:17.285
or ML, is.

00:00:17.912 --> 00:00:21.940
So, before we get too far into the details,
I want to take a minute to talk about some

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:26.580
terms that are often used interchangeably
but have certain differences.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:36.443
Terms like “artificial intelligence”,
“machine learning”, and even “deep learning”.

00:00:37.471 --> 00:00:45.530
So, at the highest level, AI is defined as
leveraging computers or machines to mimic

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:49.896
the problem-solving and the decision-making
capabilities of the human mind. 

00:00:50.454 --> 00:00:58.260
And machine learning is a subset within AI that's more
focused on the use of various self-learning

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:02.854
algorithms that derive knowledge from data
in order to predict outcomes.

00:01:03.603 --> 00:01:08.850
And then, finally, deep learning is a further
subset within even machine learning, and deep

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:13.280
learning is often thought of as scalable machine
learning because it automates a lot of the

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:19.170
feature extraction process away and eliminates
the some of the human intervention involved

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:22.155
to enable the use of some really, really big
data sets.

00:01:22.556 --> 00:01:26.329
But for today we'll focus just on machine
learning, so we'll get rid of the other two

00:01:26.329 --> 00:01:30.466
and dive one level deeper and talk about the
different types of machine learning.

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:40.131
Ok. So, the first type that we have is called
“supervised learning”. 

00:01:42.639 --> 00:01:49.027
And this is when we use labeled data sets to train algorithms
to classify data or predict outcomes.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:55.490
And when I say labeled, I mean that the rows
in the data set are labeled, tagged, or classified

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:58.899
in some interesting way that tells us something
about that data.

00:01:58.899 --> 00:02:04.749
So, it could be a yes or a no, or it could
be a particular category of some, you know,

00:02:04.749 --> 00:02:05.673
different attribute.

00:02:06.509 --> 00:02:10.509
OK, so how do we apply supervised machine
learning techniques?

00:02:10.666 --> 00:02:14.980
Well, this really depends on your particular
use-case.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:20.159
We could be using a classification model 

00:02:23.504 --> 00:02:29.185
which recognizes and groups ideas or objects into predefined categories.

00:02:29.516 --> 00:02:36.377
An example of this in the real world is with
customer retention.

00:02:39.216 --> 00:02:43.310
So, if you're in the business of managing
customers, one of your goals is typically

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:48.709
minimizing and identifying customer churn,
right, which are customers that no longer

00:02:48.709 --> 00:02:54.730
buy a particular product or service, and we
want to avoid churn because it's almost always

00:02:54.730 --> 00:03:00.280
more costly to acquire a new customer than
it is to retain an existing one, right?

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:07.900
So, if we have historical data for the customer,
like their activity - whether they churned

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:12.871
or not, right - we can build a classification
model using supervised machine learning, and

00:03:12.871 --> 00:03:18.599
our labeled data set that will help us identify
customers that are about to churn, and then

00:03:18.599 --> 00:03:20.787
allow us to take action to retain them.

00:03:22.111 --> 00:03:28.098
OK, so the other type of supervised learning
is regression.

00:03:31.407 --> 00:03:36.340
Now, this is when we build an equation using
various input values with their specific weights

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:40.815
determined by the overall value of their impact
on the outcome.

00:03:41.285 --> 00:03:45.890
And we use these to generate an estimate for
an output value.

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:48.620
So, let me give you another example here.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:56.409
So, airlines rely heavily on machine learning,
and they use regression techniques to accurately

00:03:56.409 --> 00:04:00.019
predict how much they should be charging for
a particular flight, right?

00:04:00.019 --> 00:04:05.654
So, they use various input factors like, you
know, days before departure, the day of the week,

00:04:05.654 --> 00:04:13.459
the departure, the destination to use
these to predict an accurate dollar value

00:04:13.459 --> 00:04:18.000
for how much they should be charging for a
specific flight that will maximize their revenue.

00:04:19.385 --> 00:04:25.587
OK, so now let's move on to the second type
of machine learning which is 

00:04:27.695 --> 00:04:32.151
“unsupervised learning”.

00:04:36.505 --> 00:04:42.030
OK, so this is when we use machine learning
algorithms to analyze and cluster unlabeled

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:47.810
data sets, and this method helps us discover
hidden patterns or groupings without the need

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:49.941
for human intervention, right?

00:04:49.941 --> 00:04:53.220
So, we're using unlabeled data here.

00:04:53.603 --> 00:04:57.885
So, again, let's talk about the different
techniques for unsupervised learning.

00:04:58.181 --> 00:05:02.110
One method is “clustering”. 

00:05:05.332 --> 00:05:10.003
And a real-world
example of this is when organizations try to do 

00:05:10.229 --> 00:05:13.030
customer segmentation.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:15.733
Right. 

00:05:15.733 --> 00:05:21.490
So, when businesses try to do effective marketing
it's really critical that they really understand

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:25.583
who their customers are, right, so that they
can connect with them in the most relevant way.

00:05:25.991 --> 00:05:31.810
And, oftentimes, it's not obvious or clear
how certain customers are similar to or different

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:37.980
from one another, right, and clustering algorithms
can help take into account a variety of information

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:42.869
on the customer like their purchase history,

00:05:44.869 --> 00:05:49.673
you know, their social media activity, or website activity, 

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:58.599
could be their geography, and much more, to group similar customers

00:05:58.599 --> 00:06:03.860
into buckets so that we can send them more
relevant offers, provide them better customer

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:07.925
service, and be more targeted with our marketing
efforts.

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:09.557
Ok.

00:06:10.115 --> 00:06:14.018
And the last point I want to touch on
for unsupervised learning is

00:06:15.429 --> 00:06:18.849
called “dimensionality reduction”.

00:06:21.305 --> 00:06:26.720
So, we won't discuss this in detail in this
video, but this refers to techniques that

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:32.220
reduce the number of input variables in a
data set so we don't let some redundant parameters

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:35.650
over represent the impact on the on the outcome.

00:06:37.653 --> 00:06:38.153
Ok.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:42.823
So the last type of machine learning I
want to talk about today is called

00:06:43.189 --> 00:06:45.612
“reinforcement learning”.

00:06:46.657 --> 00:06:54.304
Now, this is a form of semi-supervised learning
where we typically have an agent or system

00:06:56.586 --> 00:06:58.673
take actions 

00:07:01.669 --> 00:07:03.963
in an environment.

00:07:05.552 --> 00:07:10.632
Now the environment will then either reward
the agent for correct moves, 

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:15.107
or punish it for incorrect moves. Right.

00:07:15.194 --> 00:07:19.908
And, through many iterations of this, we can
teach a system a particular task.

00:07:20.465 --> 00:07:25.129
Now a great example of this method in the
real world is with self-driving cars.

00:07:25.129 --> 00:07:28.569
So, autonomous driving has several factors,
right?

00:07:28.569 --> 00:07:32.871
There's the speed limit, there are drivable
zones, there are collisions, and so on.

00:07:33.202 --> 00:07:39.520
So, we can use forms of reinforcement learning
to teach a system how to drive by avoiding

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:42.691
collisions, following the speed limit, and
so on.

00:07:43.875 --> 00:07:47.214
OK, so we covered many topics today, but you know, 

00:07:47.214 --> 00:07:49.161
we've barely scratched the surface of each one.

00:07:49.562 --> 00:07:53.949
If you found any one particular aspect of
machine learning interesting, I encourage

00:07:53.949 --> 00:07:58.150
you to dive deeper and learn more about it.
And if you want to know what are some of the

00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:03.080
common machine learning algorithms and how
to leverage them in data science, please check

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:04.659
out some of the links in the description.

